  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nA low pressure system along the West Coast will maintain stronger\nonshore flow at the surface and drier southwest flow aloft \nthrough Friday, with continuing near all-time record sea surface \ntemperatures over the coastal waters. High temperatures for the \nmountains and inland valleys will remain as much as 5 to 10 \ndegrees below average while high temperatures near the coast \nremain around average. High temperatures will range from the mid \n70s near the coast to the mid to upper 80s to lower 90s for the \nInland Empire the lower deserts around 100. Low temperatures will \nbe a few to around 5 degrees below average for inland areas. Low \ntemperatures for coastal areas will be mostly in the 60s, in the \nmid 50s to lower 60s for the valleys, in the 40s to mid 50s for \nthe mountains, and in the upper 60s to upper 70s for the lower \ndeserts. Patchy night and morning low clouds will extend into \nportions of the western valleys at times.\n\nTropical Storm Marie continues to strengthen and is expected to\nbecome a hurricane in the next 12-24 hours while continuing on a \ntrack toward the northwest. In the ensemble guidance, there \ncontinues to be a gradual slow shift toward the south and west \nwith the highest track density. There continues to be somewhat \ngreater spread in the track guidance once Marie reaches the \nlongitude of Point Conception. \n\nThe most likely scenario remains that the core of Marie will\nremain well to the southwest of the area. Swell from Hurricane\nMarie is expected to bring an increase in surf to southern\nCalifornia beaches during the weekend with the biggest surf for \nbeaches in Orange and San Diego Counties from Oceanside northward.\n\nEven with the relatively drier solutions, those with the core of \nMarie remaining well to the southwest, increase precipitable water\nvalues to more than one inch over the weekend. At the drier end \nof the guidance, the NBM 10th percentile precipitable water value \nat San Diego increases from around 1 inch for early Saturday \nmorning to 1.7 inches for early Sunday morning with the\ncorresponding 50th percentile values at the same times 1.5 and 2 \ninches.\n\nThe low pressure system near the West Coast could draw some of the\nmid and high level moisture from Marie into southern California\nand the Great Basin bringing a chance of showers and a slight \nchance of thunderstorms. Over time from Saturday into Sunday, \nwhere that might occur would be moving slowly northward with time \nwith the rainfall potential increasing with time as well with the \nincreasing moisture content of the atmosphere. However, there \nremains larger spread in the details. \n\nNights should be warmer for the holiday weekend. High temperatures\nfor some inland areas could be a little cooler depending on the\namount of moisture influx and cloud coverage.",
